[{:db/id 17592186162093 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-642db6cf-d41e-5a55-a66e-30cab01320f7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162351 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $9a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 16 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}72 & -36 \\\\ -36 & 18\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}72 & -36 & 20 \\\\ -36 & 18 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}72 & -36 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{18}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{18}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n9a+\\frac{b}{c}=9\\cdot \\frac{5}{18}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1}=3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159308 :answer-field/correct 17592186159310 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-642db6cf-d41e-5a55-a66e-30cab01320f7" :question/id #uuid "642db6cf-d41e-5a55-a66e-30cab01320f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-137924" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 16 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}18 & -36 \\\\ -36 & 72\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}27 \\\\ -54\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -36 & 27 \\\\ -36 & 72 & -54\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}18 & -36 & 27 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+\\frac{b}{c}=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{2}{1}=5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fc00c000-8569-5a9b-b4f0-7026f8c3241d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fc00c000-8569-5a9b-b4f0-7026f8c3241d" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc00c000-8569-5a9b-b4f0-7026f8c3241d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8eb217f8-2e73-5566-a846-de9689a469fb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bc30efa1-ce98-5d2b-b5f5-b28ca6d26bf0" "answer-8eb217f8-2e73-5566-a846-de9689a469fb" "answer-10b1479a-f401-5be2-8d54-998ace998ee3" "answer-95c93c03-292b-584d-aa3a-7dff77290397" "answer-df31033e-dd1d-5f18-81f3-78e9e429df55"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc30efa1-ce98-5d2b-b5f5-b28ca6d26bf0" :answer/id #uuid "bc30efa1-ce98-5d2b-b5f5-b28ca6d26bf0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8eb217f8-2e73-5566-a846-de9689a469fb" :answer/id #uuid "8eb217f8-2e73-5566-a846-de9689a469fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10b1479a-f401-5be2-8d54-998ace998ee3" :answer/id #uuid "10b1479a-f401-5be2-8d54-998ace998ee3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95c93c03-292b-584d-aa3a-7dff77290397" :answer/id #uuid "95c93c03-292b-584d-aa3a-7dff77290397" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df31033e-dd1d-5f18-81f3-78e9e429df55" :answer/id #uuid "df31033e-dd1d-5f18-81f3-78e9e429df55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-f2ca585c-f31d-5154-8e11-9d2a532a2dd5" :activity/id #uuid "f2ca585c-f31d-5154-8e11-9d2a532a2dd5" :activity/title "Weighted Least-Squares" :activity/type :activity.t